WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.768
You wipe the sweat off your brow as you try to breathe more deeply.

2
00:00:09.689 --> 00:00:13.032
But the air is heavy, still, and hot, and you're finding it difficult.

3
00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:15.934
Your body is covered in perspiration.

4
00:00:17.015 --> 00:00:22.080
As you close your eyes, your ears pick up hundreds of different sounds, from the cawing of toucans

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.444
up above in the treetops to the rustling of hundreds of insects on the ground below.

6
00:00:27.708 --> 00:00:34.714
A smile slowly creeps across your face as you remember where you are: the Amazon Rain Forest—otherwise known

7
00:00:34.794 --> 00:00:36.295
as the lungs of Mother Earth!

8
00:00:37.516 --> 00:00:40.859
Why is the Amazon Rain Forest often called the lungs of our planet?

9
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.903
The Amazon converts carbon dioxide produced on Earth into oxygen.

10
00:00:46.964 --> 00:00:52.128
In fact, it releases approximately 20 percent of the oxygen for the entire planet.

11
00:00:53.384 --> 00:00:58.969
It is able to do this because the rain forest is a huge, concentrated area of trees, which,

12
00:00:59.009 --> 00:01:00.770
like all plants, produce oxygen.

13
00:01:02.111 --> 00:01:06.795
The oxygen released from the rain forest is used by people and other species around the globe.

14
00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:12.960
The Amazon Rain Forest is one of the most significant biospheres on our planet.

15
00:01:14.001 --> 00:01:15.422
So what is a rain forest?

16
00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:18.485
What makes the Amazon Rain Forest so special?

17
00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:21.848
And what can we do to help preserve this resource?

18
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.784
A rain forest is exactly what the name suggests: a forest in which rain falls almost constantly.

19
00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:37.791
The average annual rainfall in the Amazon is between 130 to 445 centimeters, while the daily temperature ranges

20
00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.873
from 20 to 34 degrees Celsius.

21
00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:45.297
Humidity within the forest hovers between 77 and 88 percent.

22
00:01:46.558 --> 00:01:48.920
These conditions make it ideal for plants to grow.

23
00:01:50.012 --> 00:01:53.253
It is no wonder Darwin called the Amazon "nature's hothouse."

24
00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:59.396
The rain forest is made up of distinct layers that contribute to the climate inside.

25
00:02:00.616 --> 00:02:04.818
Emergent trees grow to fifty meters and thrust out above the rest of the forest.

26
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:10.841
The next level of plant life is known as the canopy, and it captures the vast majority of

27
00:02:10.861 --> 00:02:11.881
the sunlight and rain.

28
00:02:12.881 --> 00:02:17.763
Trees in the canopy grow thirty to forty meters tall, with branches that spread out to form a

29
00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:21.078
dense cover of foliage that the sunlight rarely penetrates.

30
00:02:22.278 --> 00:02:27.823
As a result plants such as younger trees and shrubs growing under the canopy—in a layer called the

31
00:02:27.923 --> 00:02:31.025
understory—have a hard time getting enough sun to grow.

32
00:02:32.326 --> 00:02:37.911
On the other hand, the canopy prevents moisture, which all plants and animals need, from evaporating.

33
00:02:39.852 --> 00:02:45.316
The scarcity of light results in a fierce fight among the species living in the rain forest.

34
00:02:45.336 --> 00:02:49.619
It also means that the majority of movement and activity is seen at the top layer.

35
00:02:50.759 --> 00:02:58.404
Rainbow-colored macaws and harpy eagles fly overhead while three-toed sloths hang by their feet, spider monkeys jump from

36
00:02:58.464 --> 00:03:01.566
branch to branch, and huge snakes hang from tree limbs.

37
00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:05.828
Many animals spend their entire lives high above the ground.

38
00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:11.572
Even though the wildlife lives mainly in the canopy, the floor of the forest is not dead.

39
00:03:12.636 --> 00:03:19.724
On the contrary scientists are still cataloging the insects that inhabit the forest floor—some experts claim as many

40
00:03:19.764 --> 00:03:21.546
as thirty million species exist.

41
00:03:22.787 --> 00:03:29.234
Many of these insects are scavengers, helping in the decomposition of dead plants and animals and providing nutrients

42
00:03:29.294 --> 00:03:29.995
for new growth.

43
00:03:31.637 --> 00:03:34.640
The Amazon has long been viewed as an economic gold mine.

44
00:03:35.368 --> 00:03:41.450
It is no wonder that people are constantly devising ways to exploit the Amazon Rain Forest's natural resources

45
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.850
for financial advantage.

46
00:03:44.531 --> 00:03:48.972
People originally became aware of this forest's importance because of its rubber trees.

47
00:03:50.172 --> 00:03:53.393
In the nineteenth century, rubber was found only in this area.

48
00:03:54.513 --> 00:03:58.254
In time, however, the land itself would become the desirable commodity.

49
00:03:59.435 --> 00:04:03.956
In the 1960s the Brazilian government put a tragic policy into effect.

50
00:04:05.064 --> 00:04:10.048
The government built the Trans-Amazon Highway and encouraged people to settle deeper in the forest.

51
00:04:11.229 --> 00:04:14.011
Many poorer people were lured by offers of free land.

52
00:04:15.192 --> 00:04:21.657
Settlers, ignorant of the precarious balance within the rain forest, began a slash-and-burn policy that continues to this

53
00:04:21.697 --> 00:04:27.562
day. They cut down all the vegetation in an area, and then burn it in order to clear

54
00:04:27.602 --> 00:04:29.564
the land for farming or cattle grazing.

55
00:04:31.428 --> 00:04:37.451
Sadly the infertile soil in the rain forest is not suitable for agriculture and is exhausted of its

56
00:04:37.511 --> 00:04:38.952
nutrients within a few years.

57
00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:44.735
As a result people have to constantly clear more and more land in order to survive.

58
00:04:45.836 --> 00:04:50.718
In 1997 satellites recorded 45,000 fires in the Amazon.

59
00:04:51.839 --> 00:04:57.482
These fires release carbon dioxide into the air and reduce the number of plants available to replace carbon

60
00:04:57.502 --> 00:05:01.104
dioxide with oxygen, doubly worsening the greenhouse effect.

61
00:05:02.132 --> 00:05:06.073
However, the fastest-growing threat to the Amazon Rain Forest is logging.

62
00:05:07.134 --> 00:05:10.315
Often huge trees are linked with many smaller trees by vines.

63
00:05:11.255 --> 00:05:16.516
When a tree is cut down, the network of vines drags several other trees down with it, causing

64
00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:17.917
unnecessary destruction.

65
00:05:18.997 --> 00:05:23.719
Added to this tragedy is the fact that logging companies can log much faster than trees can recover.

66
00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:27.840
Logged areas need at least seventy years to grow back.

67
00:05:29.808 --> 00:05:37.412
Furthermore logging endangers the rain forest's delicate ecosystem because it disturbs the intricate web of dependence among species.

68
00:05:38.573 --> 00:05:44.256
As scientists began classifying the planet's species, they discovered that half of them were found in the Amazon

69
00:05:44.616 --> 00:05:46.297
and other tropical rain forests.

70
00:05:47.238 --> 00:05:54.001
Biologists and botanists alike warn that continued indiscriminate logging will result in the loss of many species that

71
00:05:54.041 --> 00:05:58.344
have never been classified, and whose extinctions will have far-reaching effects.

72
00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:06.484
For instance, plants called bromeliads, many of which grow on trees, have cup-shaped leaves capable of holding pools

73
00:06:06.524 --> 00:06:07.965
of water far above the ground.

74
00:06:09.265 --> 00:06:13.208
These pools become a feeding and breeding ground for birds, animals, and insects.

75
00:06:14.468 --> 00:06:19.171
If the trees on which bromeliads grow are cut down, the creatures that rely on the pools of

76
00:06:19.211 --> 00:06:22.252
water have to struggle that much more for their survival.

77
00:06:24.033 --> 00:06:29.496
Modern medicine also relies a great deal on the plants found within the Amazon Rain Forest.

78
00:06:29.516 --> 00:06:34.999
One study shows that 25 percent of the medicines used in the United States and other countries come

79
00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:36.180
from rain forest plants.

80
00:06:37.401 --> 00:06:42.964
For example, curare, a medicine derived from a vine, has been useful in cardiac surgery.

81
00:06:44.025 --> 00:06:47.867
Another drug, called quinine, is a key ingredient in the treatment of malaria.

82
00:06:48.968 --> 00:06:54.711
The study also states that 70 percent of the plants shown to be effective against cancer have been

83
00:06:54.751 --> 00:06:57.912
found in tropical rain forests.

84
00:06:57.932 --> 00:07:05.418
Fewer than 1 percent of the world's estimated 250,000 tropical plants have been analyzed, so scientists are frantically

85
00:07:05.478 --> 00:07:10.623
trying to collect data that may result in cures for the diseases that plague the human race.

86
00:07:12.504 --> 00:07:19.530
In addition, as developers steadily encroach on the Amazon, loss of habitat may destroy another valuable resource for

87
00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.952
medical development: the native people.

88
00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:29.421
Researchers that study the relationships between people and plants hope to be able to record traditional native treatments

89
00:07:29.481 --> 00:07:33.304
in order to find out about drugs that can be used to cure modern diseases.

90
00:07:34.405 --> 00:07:38.148
However, these native tribes are disappearing at an alarming rate.

91
00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:41.270
What can we do to combat this destruction?

92
00:07:42.531 --> 00:07:48.216
In 1987 children from Sweden started to collect money through a variety of fund-raising activities.

93
00:07:49.364 --> 00:07:54.926
They were able to raise $100,000 and buy a section of rain forest near the Costa Rican village

94
00:07:54.986 --> 00:07:55.786
of Monteverde.

95
00:07:56.946 --> 00:07:59.847
This spurred children from countries around the world to do the same.

96
00:08:00.848 --> 00:08:06.509
Within a few years, about 42,000 acres had been bought for what came to be called the Children's

97
00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:07.269
Rain Forest.

98
00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.271
Let's learn from these children and the many others working to protect the Amazon: If we combine our

99
00:08:11.291 --> 00:08:15.292
efforts, we can save this precious resource—the lungs of planet Earth!
